WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:06.460
The Marvel Cinematic Universe's first post-credits
scene almost looked into the franchise's future…

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:07.900
way into the future.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.410
At the 2019 Saturn Awards, Marvel Studios
head honcho Kevin Feige appeared in a brief

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:17.840
video clip to share an alternate version of
Iron Man's famous stinger with the crowd.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:21.680
It's probably for the best that they went
with a simplified take, because if they had

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.160
used the alternate, Marvel fans' heads would
have exploded.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:30.240
The clip is part of an apparent treasure drove
of deleted scenes from the MCU that will be

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.190
made available on the Infinity Saga box set,
and it features a very different version of

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:39.460
the speech Nick Fury gives to Tony Stark after
surprising the billionaire at his home.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:45.240
The original post-credits scene of Iron Man
introduced audiences to Fury, and clued them

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.340
in that, yes, all these Marvel movies were
going to be connected.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.940
"Mr. Stark, you've become part of a bigger
universe you just don't know it yet."

00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:01.079
Marvel comics die-hards went absolutely bananas
over the reveal of Jackson as Fury and the

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:05.940
teased possibility of an Avengers movie but
the alternate version that Feige screened

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.060
for fans would have had them freaking out
exponentially harder.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.939
Here's the alternate line of dialogue that
would have changed everything:

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:18.640
"As if gamma accidents, radioactive bug bites,
and assorted mutants weren't enough …"

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.840
Wait — what?!

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:28.090
The implication of the existence of Spider-Man
and the X-Men in the MCU's very first film,

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:31.000
no less would have blown fans out of their
seats.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.340
Fury continues ...

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:37.060
"... I have to deal with a spoiled brat who
doesn't play well with others, and who wants

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:38.880
to keep all of his toys to himself."

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.280
The "Who the hell are you?"

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:46.000
exchange between Fury and Stark is the same
as in the final version, but there's an additional

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.960
exchange that is also new:

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.160
"Well, what are we avenging?"

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:52.540
"Whatever the hell we want."

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.320
The pre-Sokovia Accords era was such an innocent
time, eh?

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.360
But back to the Fury's mind-blowing opening
line.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.990
Remember, at that time, the film rights to
the X-Men were owned by Fox Studios, who had

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.649
been busily cranking out successful films
based on these properties for the past eight

00:02:08.649 --> 00:02:09.180
years.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.819
The rights to Spider-Man were held by Sony
Pictures, which had fielded an immensely successful

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:19.260
film trilogy based on the wall-crawler, and
was a few years away from rebooting the property.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.540
Of course, the last few years have made this
alternate version of the Iron Man stinger

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.480
scene appear downright prophetic.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:30.530
Following the striking of a shared custody
deal between Sony and Marvel, Spider-Man has

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.400
enjoyed a three-year, five-picture run in
the MCU.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:38.330
Negotiations to re-up that deal have recently
hit an impasse, making the cinematic future

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:43.720
of the character a bit unclear but in 2008,
the idea that Spidey could ever appear in

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.640
a film from scrappy upstart Marvel Studios
was downright laughable.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:52.880
Likewise, the formerly Fox-owned properties
are now free and clear for use in the MCU,

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:58.740
but it took two seismic developments for that
to happen: Marvel Studios and Fox Studios

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:04.629
were both purchased by the same parent company,
the increasingly monolithic Disney.

00:03:04.629 --> 00:03:08.900
In 2009, the House of Mouse purchased Marvel
Studios for just over $4 billion dollars,

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:12.220
which turned out to be a pretty sweet deal
for a production house that would eventually

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:16.580
turn the MCU into the highest-grossing film
franchise in history.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:22.480
Then, earlier this year, Disney acquired the
film and television assets of Fox for an eye-popping

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.800
$71.3 billion dollars.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:30.140
While one of the primary purposes of the acquisition
was to secure Fox's incredibly deep television

00:03:30.159 --> 00:03:34.939
library to bolster its upcoming streaming
service Disney+, it goes without saying that

00:03:34.939 --> 00:03:39.080
bringing all of those legacy characters home
to Marvel was some pretty fantastic icing

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.080
slathered liberally on that cake.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.879
But in 2008, there would have been no reason
to think that the X-Men and the Avengers could

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.209
ever play in the same cinematic sandbox.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:53.000
At any rate, it's fascinating to see what
might have been and even more so to think

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:56.939
what else Feige and company will be dragging
out of their vault to share with us for the

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.200
Infinity Saga's box set release.

00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:03.840
It has yet to be announced when the collection
will hit store shelves, but we'll be all over

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:04.880
it when it does.

